A||||12||G25C||Serious shake and knock from front left tire
Z||000000||G25C||09-01-99||07:55 AM||||last night as i left work to pick my daughter up, i heard a slight whistleing noise that would come and go.  Then my left front tire started to thump.  So i thought "ok, i gotta flat tire".<p>I pulled over and i have NO flat tires!  So i got back in and drove down the road.  Then all of a sudden, the l/f tire jerked hard and began to clunk and wobble.  I had to slow down to 20 miles an hour and it still mades a grinding bumping noise and feel.<p>I stopped again and checked undernieth the front to see if something was broken.  Nothing<p>I got to my sisters doing 20m/h and preying that the tire was not going to fly off (cause thats what it felt like it was gonna do).<p>I took the l/f tire off and looked at all the control bars.  None are broken and all bushing are intact and in pretty decent shape.<p>So i checked the rotor, it was intack and the brakes work as good as ever.  The shock is still bolted in and the coil spring has nothing wrong with it either.<p>I checked the other side and cant find nothing wrong there either.  I babyed the car home and the whole time it was scraping and thumping and shaking and wobbling.<p>The noises i heard were whisltleing, scraping, and clunking.<p>The tires are all farily new and ive replaced the brakes.  Could this be something simple like a bad wheel bearing? or something worse.  Could it be the steering shaft that has the boots on them?<p>I hope this is an easy fix, cause my other dependable vehicle is a 86 3/4 ton 4x4 chevy and it rides like a log wagon and gets about 15miles to the gallon.  
Z||000001||G25C||09-01-99||09:37 AM||||Anyone?
Z||000002||ChadMan||09-01-99||10:08 AM||||My guess would be a completely fragged wheel bearing...when my 86 SE had a bad wheel bearing, it made a loud grinding noise that you could actually feel through your heels on the floorboard.  That was just pitted bearings and races.  If a bearing came totally apart, I could imagine hearing noises similar to what you're hearing.<p>I can't imagine hearing such terrible noises due to a ball-joint or tie-rod-end problem unless it was broken clean through - and you'd have never made it home that way!
Z||000003||lowCG||09-01-99||04:01 PM||||Grab the top of the tire,with the car on the ground,and pull it towards you,real hard,again,and again,until maybe you can hear something.If possible,have someone else(the bigger the better)do the same while you look for where the slop is.If it's a wheelbearing,you should be able to detect movement between the brake rotor,and caliper.
Z||000004||Shaun41178||09-01-99||10:28 PM||||G25C:  I live in toledo and i remember you saying you living in NW ohio.  I can come take a look at it and see if I can find what the problem is.  From what you described i would almost guess that your problem is in one of the balljoints.  Perhaps the stud broke in the spindle and went undetected when you took everything apart.  Or perhaps something is worng with yor spring in someway perhaps it shifted or something and isn't sitting where it should.  These are just some suggestions.  If you like I could come take a look at it Fri.  Give me a time that would be good for you and I will see if I can do it.  however I have school till about 3;30 just to let you know.  You can email if you want instead of replying to the forum.  shaun41178@aol.com is my email address.  
Z||000005||batboy||09-02-99||04:45 AM||||Sometimes it's hard to diagnose a problem at a desk sitting behind a computer monitor, but in this case, I immediately thought ball joint or wheel bearing. I sort of ruled ball joints out because they usually separate which is extremely obvious when it does that, the tire tilts at a bad angle. I vote for a wheel bearing, they make lots of noise when they go bad.
Z||000006||G25C||09-02-99||09:55 PM||||Thanks all, im going to check that ball joint first and see what i come up with.  My brother looked at it when i was at work and he said he was able to move the caliper around a bit.  He said that he didnt know if it had a bearing in there (he's a mustang nut) but he said if there is, he would guess that would be the problem.<p>....Shaun, thanks for the offer but i work afternoons and wouldnt be home.  (i work for at a Ford factory).
Z||000007||batboy||09-03-99||04:48 AM||||The front brake rotor has both inner and outer wheel bearings and an inner dust seal.
Z||000008||theogre||09-03-99||02:31 PM||||GM calipers should float rather freely. Very freely compaired to a Ford unit. Unless the pads are gone, this is not the source of your problem.
Z||000009||G25C||09-03-99||10:07 PM||||It was the outer wheel bearing.  It the races had seperated allowing the bearings to slip out.  Luckely nothing was damaged from it and it is a couple dollar quick fix.<p>Thanks guys for your help
Z||000010||batboy||09-03-99||10:54 PM||||If that outer bearing went bad, I'd replace the inner one too. Cheap insurance.
Z||000011||theogre||09-04-99||04:00 PM||||ALWAYS, ALWYAS, replace wheel bearings as a set! You may not be able to see it, but the inner was likely damaged when the outer failed.
Z||000012||Harry||09-04-99||06:02 PM||||With a Fiero,<br>You have a much lighter car and wheel bearing problems do not show sooner evidence like full size cars do (ie, squeeling ).<br>You should have also noticed a tendancy of the steering having a loose feeling from the change of alignment<br>
